                    warn_invest_range:      `Generative-output investment must be between €0k and €1,800k.`,
                    warn_override_range:    `Human-override strength must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_conviction_range:  `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_win_right:     `<strong>Right win condition.</strong> Against an insurgent eating share, you win by being noticed and remembered as Aurore — distinctiveness, the exact metric you fell 18% below last spring. Now protect it.`,
                    coach_r1_polish:        `<strong>Polish is table stakes, not the win.</strong> The category is already polished. The insurgent is taking share on distinctiveness; flawless-but-forgettable loses the line. Win on being remembered, not merely clean.`,
                    coach_r1_speed:         `<strong>Speed alone is a trap.</strong> You will hit the shelf — and be indistinguishable from last year. Against six points of share loss, on-time and forgettable still loses. The clock matters, but it is not the win condition.`,
                    coach_r1_price:         `<strong>Price is the insurgent's game, not yours.</strong> A mid-market brand defending an €88M line on price messaging races to the bottom. You win on distinctiveness; let the creative carry it.`,
                    coach_r1_tagging:       `<strong>Asset tagging is the safest task to automate, not the riskiest.</strong> Mislabelling the media library costs you minutes, not the brand. The most damaging place to mis-balance is the creative core — concept and voice.`,
                    coach_r1_scalable:      `<strong>That is scalable production, not the creative core.</strong> Variants and localisation are exactly where AI is supposed to help. Getting the balance wrong there costs throughput; getting it wrong on concept and voice costs distinctiveness.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named distinctiveness as the win condition AND located the creative core — concept and brand voice — as the place you must not flatten. That is the lens that makes the rest of your deployment decisions land correctly.`,
                    coach_r1_lowsuit:       `<strong>Vague on where AI belongs.</strong> If the team cannot separate amplify-with-AI tasks from protect-for-humans tasks, you will repeat last spring — generative tools creeping onto the creative core. Get the map sharp before you deploy.`,
                    coach_r2_autocore:      `<strong>You handed the core concept and brand voice to generative AI.</strong> Fast — and exactly what tanked last spring, when distinctiveness fell 18% as the machine regressed your brand to the category mean. You are defending on distinctiveness and just automated the one thing that makes you distinctive. The core is the wrong place for generative AI.`,
                    coach_r2_nongencore:    `<strong>Non-generative AI cannot author the core.</strong> Analytics and prediction tell you what will land; they cannot write the brand voice. Put humans (or human-AI co-creation) on the concept — the core needs judgement and meaning, not a model.`,
                    coach_r2_humanvariants: `<strong>Hand-building every variant burns the clock.</strong> Resizing one idea into hundreds of placements is precisely where generative AI absorbs work without touching the concept. Free your humans for the core and let AI scale the variants.`,
                    coach_r2_nongenvariants:`<strong>Non-generative AI does not produce variants.</strong> It analyses; it does not create. Variant and resize production is generative-AI territory — that is where the throughput to hit week five comes from.`,
                    coach_r2_analytics_right:`<strong>Exactly right on analytics.</strong> Audience modelling and performance prediction are non-generative — they never touch a pixel, they tell you which concept will land. You protected the creative core and still sharpened your targeting.`,
                    coach_r2_humananalytics:`<strong>You are hand-modelling audiences.</strong> That is slow and weaker than the tool, and it is non-generative — it never threatens the creative. Let the analytics engine sharpen your targeting while humans own the concept.`,
                    coach_r2_genanalytics:  `<strong>Generative AI is the wrong tool for prediction.</strong> Audience modelling and performance prediction are analysis, not content generation. Using a generative model to do non-generative work conflates the two — the classic deployment error.`,
                    coach_r2_overinvest:    `<strong>Over-spending on generation flattens, it doesn't sharpen.</strong> Past a point, pouring budget into more generated output just floods the campaign with mean-regressed variants. Throughput has a sweet spot; buy distinctiveness with human judgement, not more volume.`,
                    coach_r2_misinvest:     `<strong>You invested heavily in generation but pointed it nowhere useful.</strong> With no production task in a generative or co-creation mode, the spend cannot buy throughput — and risks pushing generation onto the core. Aim the budget at variants and localisation.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>That is a complementary deployment matrix.</strong> Humans (or co-creation) own the concept, generative AI scales the variants, and non-generative analytics sharpens targeting without touching a creative asset. AI for scale and analysis, humans for meaning.`,
                    coach_r3_humanfirst:    `<strong>Human-first sequencing protects the core.</strong> Setting concept and voice by hand, then letting AI scale variants and localisation, is the orchestration that amplifies rather than flattens — divergence and scale from the machine, meaning from the humans.`,
                    coach_r3_aifirst:       `<strong>AI-first, human-last is how you flatten.</strong> If the machine generates everything and humans only polish at the end, the brand voice is already regressed to the mean before a person sees it — and a light edit cannot rescue distinctiveness. It also raises the rights risk you have not yet gated.`,
                    coach_r3_parallel:      `<strong>No defined order means no orchestration.</strong> Running human and AI work in parallel and merging at the end loses the point — the value is in the sequence, humans framing the work the machine then scales. Merging blind costs both time and coherence.`,
                    coach_r3_gatecaught:    `<strong>The review gate just earned its place.</strong> A generated asset was drifting toward a rights/resemblance problem and your before-print gate caught it — the same near-miss that triggered last quarter's legal review, stopped before it reached out-of-home across Paris.`,
                    coach_r3_nogate:        `<strong>You shipped fast with no rights gate.</strong> A generated visual closely resembles the insurgent's hero image — the same near-miss as last quarter, now live on out-of-home across Paris. The relaunch headline is a cease-and-desist, not the product. Governance is part of the workflow, not optional.`,
                    coach_r3_nooverride:    `<strong>Almost no human override means AI output ships raw.</strong> Without human selection, refinement and brand-sense at each gate, the machine's mean-regressed output goes out unedited — distinctiveness leaks away exactly where judgement should defend it.`,
                    coach_r3_flatratio:     `<strong>A flat 60/40 split is one-ratio thinking.</strong> Concept needs ~95% human and variant production needs ~90% AI. A single ratio over-humanises the scalable work and over-automates the meaningful work. The balance is per task, not a dial.`,
                    coach_r3_pertask:       `<strong>Per-task calibration is the central move.</strong> Concept stays almost fully human, variants run almost fully on AI, each task tuned to where it adds value — sequenced human-first and gated before print. That is human-centered orchestration done right.`,
                    coach_r4_diag_right:    `<strong>That is the right recalibration.</strong> Keep the creative core human, scale production with generative AI, sharpen targeting with non-generative analytics, and tune the balance per task. You are answering the board's question instead of repeating last spring's mistake.`,
                    coach_r4_moreauto:      `<strong>More automation is the wrong lesson.</strong> If distinctiveness slipped, the answer is not to hand even more to the machine that regresses your brand to the mean. That is precisely the over-automation post-mortem the board is afraid of.`,
                    coach_r4_lessai:        `<strong>Pulling AI out entirely overcorrects.</strong> Banning generative AI forfeits the throughput that hit your deadline and the analytics that sharpened targeting. The lesson is not less AI — it is AI in the right places, humans in the core.`,
                    coach_r4_ignore:        `<strong>"We shipped on time" is not the scorecard.</strong> On time and forgettable still loses the line to an insurgent eating six points of share. Orchestration has to serve the creative outcome, not just the calendar.`,
                    coach_r4_disclose:      `<strong>Disclosing AI use is responsible deployment.</strong> Transparency with the brand-safety board is part of governing generative work — it pre-empts the disclosure questions generated assets raise and protects the relaunch from a later surprise.`,
                    coach_r4_quiet:         `<strong>Staying quiet on AI use invites the surprise you fear.</strong> Generated outputs carry disclosure obligations; hiding them turns a manageable statement into a brand-safety incident if it surfaces later. Disclose it on your own terms.`,
                    coach_r4_complementary: `<strong>Complementarity is the operating model.</strong> AI for divergence, scale and analysis; humans for judgement, meaning and brand sense — calibrated per task. That defends the studio's mandate to lead with AI without handing the board another flattening.`,
                    coach_r4_pitchauto:     `<strong>"Automate the studio" is the pitch that loses the mandate.</strong> Shrinking the human team and leaning on the machine is how you regress to the mean and repeat the −18%. The board funded a studio that orchestrates AI, not one that surrenders to it.`,
                    coach_r4_pitchhuman:    `<strong>Banning AI throws away the throughput that hit your date.</strong> A human-only studio cannot ship this scope in five weeks and abandons the analytics that sharpen targeting. The answer is orchestration, not prohibition.`,

